00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:33.280
before the deadline runs in May.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:34.460
But enough about the legal minutia

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:36.650
that perhaps only I care about.

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:38.620
Let's dig into the meat and potatoes

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:42.300
of Tulsi's defamation claim
against Hillary Clinton.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.270
As you may know, defamation
is an area of law

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:46.550
that gives people a remedy
when someone says something

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:49.700
that is harmful to a person's
reputation or livelihood,

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:51.480
and that is, of course, false.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.040
Traditionally defamation has been

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:54.770
divided up into two categories.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:58.380
Written defamation, called
libel, that's L-I-B-E-L,

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:02.770
not liable, L-I-A-B-L-E, and slander,

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:04.710
which is spoken defamation.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:08.190
But courts see no real
distinction between the two kinds,

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:10.660
and so it's gentlemen just
referred to as defamation.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:13.070
In its most basic sense,
a statement is defamatory

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:15.860
if it tends to harm the
reputation of another,

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:18.730
as to lower him or her in the
estimation of the community,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.500
or to deter third
persons from associating,

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:23.600
or dealing with him or her.

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.020
In other words, defamation
is making some statement

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:28.460
which takes a serious toll
on someone's reputation,

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:30.040
and therefore, causes them harm.

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:31.880
As you may already know, defamation suits

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:33.540
are particularly difficult to win.

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:35.730
That's because defamation law has evolved,

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.520
and as the Supreme Court has decided

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:40.840
more and more defamation
cases, that prima facie case,

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:43.390
a plaintiff must prove,
has gotten more specific

00:08:43.390 --> 00:08:44.910
and far more challenging.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:46.820
But prior to the mid-20th century,

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:48.480
a plaintiff in a defamation suit,

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:49.770
typically only had to prove

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:51.470
that a statement made by the defendant

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.170
was intentionally or
negligently published,

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:56.100
and that the remark defamed the plaintiff

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:57.410
in the minds of the recipients.

00:08:57.410 --> 00:08:59.910
And as for the plaintiff,
doing so was pretty easy.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:02.750
All you had to do was say,
hey, this guy called me

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:05.490
a lily livered loser, and I ain't one,

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:08.620
and bam, you could win
the defamation lawsuit.

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.530
However, a landmark Supreme Court case

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:12.270
changed defamation law forever,

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:15.170
making it harder to sue
and easier to practice

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.160
truly free speech, because you can imagine

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:20.960
in the prior example, that's
a recipe for stifling speech.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.950
When the Supreme Court
decided the seminal case

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:25.040
of New York Times versus Sullivan,

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.970
the pendulum swung much
more towards the defendant,

00:09:27.970 --> 00:09:29.810
and made it much harder for plaintiffs.

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:31.890
In that case the New York Times
published an advertisement

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:34.680
asking for donations to
defend Martin Luther King.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:37.140
The ad criticized the police
in Montgomery, Alabama

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:39.410
for their actions during
the Civil Rights protest

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:41.380
in the city, and the ad contained

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:43.490
several minor factual inaccuracies,

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:45.270
such as misstating the number of times

00:09:45.270 --> 00:09:47.050
that Dr. King was arrested.

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:49.320
Now the Montgomery Public
Safety Commissioner,

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.310
L.B. Sullivan, who was
not mentioned in the ad,

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:55.150
took it as a personal attack,
and he asked the Times

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.670
to retract the information,
and they refused.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.470
Then Sullivan sued the New York Times,

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:01.350
and several African American protesters

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.420
under the Alabama libel law,

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.080
and he was actually able to
win a huge verdict at trial,

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:08.410
and as a result, the defendants appealed.

00:10:08.410 --> 00:10:09.730
Eventually, the case went all the way up

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.480
to the US Supreme Court,
and the Supreme Court

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:13.810
issued a unanimous decision,

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.630
stating that the verdict
violated the First Amendment.

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:19.200
This decision is a notable
defense of freedom of the press.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.150
Before the Sullivan case,
Southern politicians had filed

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:24.930
nearly $300 million in libel actions

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:26.730
against news organizations.

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:29.880
This was a coordinated effort
to use defamation lawsuits

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.600
to suppress coverage of police brutality,

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.660
discrimination, and the
civil rights movement,

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:36.870
but the court's decision
announced a new test

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.120
for defamation claims
involving public figures.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.000
When allegedly defamatory statements

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:43.870
involve a public figure,
it's not enough to say that

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:47.830
they are false, and that they
were negligently published,

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:49.990
the public figure must also
prove that the statement

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:52.690
was made with what's called actual malice,

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:54.540
and actual malice, doesn't mean

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:55.750
they really hated this person.

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:56.920
It's a legal term of art.

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:59.950
It means that the speaker must
have known, actually known,

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:02.360
that the statement they
were making was false,

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.090
or that the person made that statement

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:07.230
with reckless disregard
for its truth, or falsity.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:10.630
Those are much higher
standards than negligence,

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:12.370
and just simply not knowing

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:14.000
whether something is true or false.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:17.010
The idea was that if public
figures could sue newspapers,

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:19.530
and citizens anytime that
they were criticized,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.570
it would effectively silence criticism

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.510
and make government less transparent.

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:24.950
Now you've probably heard the statement

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.850
that truth is a defense to defamation,

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.740
and this refers to the law's requirement

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:29.770
that this statement a person

00:11:29.770 --> 00:11:31.700
is complaining about must be false.

00:11:31.700 --> 00:11:33.000
Now, why is that?

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.660
Because if the statement
is critical, but true,

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:37.410
it's not considered damaging,

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:38.510
or at least not the kind of damage

00:11:38.510 --> 00:11:41.040
that we want the court system to remedy.

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.150
Under American law, and
this is not the case

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:46.110
throughout the world, you
can only sue for defamation

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.010
if something false is said about you.

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:49.890
True things that put
you in a negative light

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:51.850
aren't grounds for a defamation suit.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:54.750
Now that's in regards to verifiable facts,

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:57.600
but free speech also gives you
the right to have opinions,

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:58.740
and to express them.

00:11:58.740 --> 00:12:01.490
So opinions, which
there's no way to validate

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:04.070
one way or another, also are not subject

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:06.660
to a defamation suit, in
part because most opinions

00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:09.060
cannot be objectively
proven true or false.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:11.670
For example, Martin Scorsese
created an internet war

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:13.420
when he wrote an Op-Ed
in the New York Times

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:16.410
about why he didn't think
superhero movies are cinema,

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:18.940
and said that they're
closer to theme parks

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:20.650
than to actual movies.

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:23.140
Now, you may agree with him or not,

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:25.230
but that's not grounds for defamation,

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:26.930
even if it was particularized against

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:28.790
one particular person, or movie.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:31.140
That's his opinion, he's
entitled to have it,

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:33.840
and he can say it without repercussions.

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:36.430
In other defamation cases,
the Supreme Court has found

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:38.070
statements to not be defamatory,

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:40.010
because even the most careless reader

00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:41.590
must have perceived that the word

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:44.350
was no more than rhetorical hyperbole.

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:47.264
So, for example when Larry
Flynt and Penthouse Magazine

00:12:47.264 --> 00:12:49.590
ran an article about Jerry Falwell

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:52.840
writing in the first person
about losing his virginity,

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:55.880
that wasn't defamation because
no one would have thought

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:58.990
it was actually Jerry
Falwell writing that article.

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:01.450
Often the curt reasons
that permitting hyperbole,

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:04.270
or satire, or parodies, that it encourages

00:13:04.270 --> 00:13:06.990
an imaginative expression
in public debate.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:08.900
In another fantastic
case that I am thrilled

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:10.720
to be able to share with you in public,

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:13.430
a newspaper article once
referred to a university official

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:16.240
as, quote, Director of Butt Licking,

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:18.750
and a Virginia court
wrote that the phrase,

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.760
Director of Butt Licking is no more

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:22.160
than rhetorical hyperbole.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:23.920
The phrase is disgusting and offensive,

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:25.340
and in extremely bad taste,

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:27.370
but it cannot reasonably be understood

00:13:27.370 --> 00:13:31.130
as stating in actual fact
about the official's job title,

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:33.080
or her conduct, or that she committed

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:34.800
a crime of moral turpitude.

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.130
So remember kids, you have
a constitutional right

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:40.260
to refer to your professor
as Professor Butt Licker,

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:42.070
but I would not recommend it.

00:13:42.070 --> 00:13:44.460
So bottom line, here is
what you need to know

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:47.180
about defamation, because the
internet is full of people

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:49.040
that do not understand defamation.

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:51.470
Number one, a true statement that damages

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:54.080
someone's reputation is not defamation.

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.700
Two, an opinion is not defamation.

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.160
Three, a statement containing hyperbole

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:01.220
is usually not defamation.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:04.630
Four, a public figure cannot
prove a false statement

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:06.950
is defamatory unless they prove the person

00:14:06.950 --> 00:14:08.700
acted with actual malice.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:10.890
And number five, and this is a big one,

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:13.870
and please if you take
anything away, remember this.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.870
Actual malice doesn't mean
I wanted to hurt someone.

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:20.210
It means the person knew
that the statement was false,

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:22.560
or acted with reckless disregard,

00:14:22.560 --> 00:14:25.640
for whether they knew it
was true or false, or not.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:27.940
And that my friend is
a very high standard,

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.150
which is why you don't
see a lot of politicians

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:31.960
filing suits for defamation.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:34.670
And generally, when you see
a politician file a suit

